WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.528
IEC 62443 is a family of standards focused on cybersecurity for industrial automation and control systems.

00:00:08.528 --> 00:00:17.544
These systems monitor or control physical processes in manufacturing, energy, water, transportation, buildings, and other operational environments.

00:00:17.544 --> 00:00:25.098
Their security must account for safety, availability, reliability, process integrity, and long equipment lifecycles.

00:00:25.098 --> 00:00:33.626
The standards provide a structured way to discuss industrial cyber risk across organizations and technical disciplines.

00:00:33.626 --> 00:00:42.155
They address security programs, system design, risk assessment, product development, technical capabilities, integration, and service delivery.

00:00:42.155 --> 00:00:49.708
IEC 62443 is therefore broader than a list of firewall settings or product features.

00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:57.749
Asset owners use the concepts to establish governance and determine what protection their operations require.

00:00:57.749 --> 00:01:03.354
Integrators translate those requirements into an architecture and commissioned system.

00:01:03.354 --> 00:01:09.933
Product suppliers build security capabilities into components and maintain secure development practices.

00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:15.537
Service providers support systems without weakening the controls around them.

00:01:15.537 --> 00:01:23.091
This division of responsibility matters because no single product can secure an industrial operation.

00:01:23.091 --> 00:01:27.233
A capable component can be configured poorly.

00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:32.838
A well-segmented system can be exposed through unmanaged remote access.

00:01:32.838 --> 00:01:40.879
Strong policy can fail if teams cannot produce evidence that controls are implemented and maintained.

00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:47.945
IEC 62443 should be treated as a risk-based, lifecycle-oriented framework for disciplined engineering.

00:01:47.945 --> 00:01:54.037
It does not eliminate uncertainty or replace knowledge of the process.

00:01:54.037 --> 00:02:05.002
Its practical value comes from connecting organizational responsibility, architecture, technical requirements, verification, and ongoing operation using a common industrial cybersecurity language.
